      stepByStepSolution: "Correct Answer: (A) No, because synthetic detergents form rich lather equally in both soft and hard water without forming insoluble scum precipitates with \$\\text{Ca}^{2+}\$ and \$\\text{Mg}^{2+}\$ ions.\n\nScientific Principle / Key Concept:\n• Hardness in water is caused by dissolved hydrogencarbonates, chlorides, or sulphates of calcium and magnesium.\n• Soaps react with \$\\text{Ca}^{2+}\$ and \$\\text{Mg}^{2+}\$ to form an insoluble white curdy precipitate called scum, failing to lather until all hardness ions are precipitated.\n• Detergents are sodium salts of alkylbenzenesulphonates or ammonium salts. The charged ends of these molecules do not form insoluble precipitates with \$\\text{Ca}^{2+}\$ and \$\\text{Mg}^{2+}\$.\n• Because detergents produce foam/lather readily in both soft and hard water, they cannot be used to distinguish hard water from soft water.\n\nDetailed Distractor & Misconception Analysis:\n• Option (B): Incorrect. Soap forms scum, not detergent.\n• Option (C): Incorrect. Detergents work effectively in hard water.\n• Option (D): Incorrect. Detergents keep ions in solution.",
